<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Yuri Dolgoruky</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1099–1157</span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Yuri Dolgoruky was Prince of Rostov-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suzdal">Suzdal</a> and, at the end of his life, Grand Prince of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kiev</a>. A son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vladimir-ii-monomakh">Vladimir Monomakh</a>, he built up the north-eastern lands of Rus, founding towns such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pereslavl-zalessky">Pereslavl-Zalessky</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuryev-polsky">Yuryev-Polsky</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> is first mentioned in 1147 as his meeting place, and he is traditionally regarded as its founder.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/yuri-dolgoruky.jpg" alt="Yuri Dolgoruky" width="320" height="405" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Царский титулярник · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Yuri_I_Dolgoruky.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
